Z||000000||Seanh||09-29-2000||07:20 AM||||I am geting very bad milage in my Fiero. I am getting about 12-10 Mpg city. I though I had a clogged cat. but no I dont think so. I really cant hear anything rattling around inside of it when I strike it. I am having trouble in the morning with my car not wanting to take off. I heard if you had a clogged cat, the car would hardly run. I have no trouble keeping it running. IMO it is lacking power. At a light, if I floor it right off, the car hesatates and then takes off. I did 0-60 in about 15 seconds. Shouldnt it do better? Any Ideas? I replaced the fuel filter and air filter recently and got a new distributor and cap, rotor, and plugs last year. I was having trouble last year and took it to a mechanic hear. He did $500 in work to it, but didnt fix the problem. He replaced  the fuel pump and new cap, rotor, and plugs, PCV valve, EGR valve, some other stuff also. Turns out he didnt put the rotor on correctly. I had that fixed and got the power back, but it was still dying at idle. I had the GM center work on it and they found a bad distrubtor and replaced it. The prongs on the dis. were bent and actually making contact with each other and stuck. They replaced the dis. and when they did, it seemed like I didnt have the same power I did before. Could having that bad dis. ruined somthing else? Plugs maybe? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||9
Z||000001||prof bobo||09-29-2000||07:52 AM||||A bad CAT can exhibit a variety of symptoms depending on how badly it's clogged, how extreme the back pressure is.  Yeah, if the guts have disintegrated and have jammed the outlet, then the car won't run -the potato in the exhaust pipe effect.  But sometimes the car will run and maybe even fairly well.  But it is likely to be sluggish, poor acceleration and no top end (feels like you're pulling a heavy trailer).  Bad gas mileage ... maybe.  Its also likely that everything ahead of it is going to get hot.  REAL HOT.  Even red hot.  So check that, run the engine a while and then feel (without actually touching) the inlet versus outlet.  If the in side is a lot hotter that the out side, you got problems.  You can also check for disintegration by putting a (thin) rag over the tail pipe for a moment while the engine is running and gun it (in neutral) a couple of times).  If the rag collects something that looks like metal glitter, you got problems.  Of course you can also take it to a muffler shop and they ought to be able to tell you if you have excessive backpressure.<P>Then check your state laws.  In many cases it's okay for the OWNER to remove the CAT and replace it with a straight pipe.  It will be rather loud though (even with a muffler).  When it happened to me I cut it off, drove a metal rod through the guts and emptied it out so it was just the metal shell, welded new mounting tubes on it and clamped it back.  I've also seen people replace it with a glass pack muffler.<P>BTW, if your CAT is clogged you probably cooked your O2 sensor and will have to replace it (no big deal since you'll be under the car getting your eyes full of dirt and rust anyway).||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-29-2000||09:19 AM||||Federally it is illegal for anyone to remove/disable an emission control device.<P>A bad catalyst could cause this. If the dealer didn't get the base timing set right it could cause problems to. vacuum leaks anyplace would cause idle problems. a problem with MAP or MAT sensor will kill power, especially when you punch it. (ECM uses MAP to calculate Vacuum Advance for timing. MAT is used with O2 to fine tune F/A ratio.)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||09-29-2000||09:31 AM||||If I did have a bad sensor somwhere, wouldnt my check engine light come on? I recently checked and there are no codes present. These sensors and pretty cheap right? Should I just give it a try and replace them one at a time to see if it has any effect? I think I should check the cat. first off, then go for timeing and sensors. How can I check the timeing myself and what equipment do I need? Also, I tried spraying carb cleaner in my intake and the car seemed to run better afer that, but now its back to its usally condition. Can I take the intake off and clean it out completely? Thanks for the help.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||09-29-2000||09:36 AM||||Forgot to mention, My car has an aftermarket cat. the reason I know this is because its not welded on. It has a clamp on each end. Maybe I should just try taking it off and checking it?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||09-29-2000||10:26 PM||||Hey, I guess I lied about the ECM not having any codes. I just checked it again, and a code 33 was there, bad MAP sensor. Wish I would have done this sooner. How much are MAP sensors? Where are they located? Could a clogged cat cause this map sensor to go bad? THanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||09-30-2000||02:07 PM||||Wouldn't a map sensor be located in your glove compartment?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The MAP sensor sits on a bracket on the right (passenger) side of the plenum.||||reg||5
